        {
            "timestamp": 1560293945,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Benjamin Fair",
                "email": "benjaminfair@google.com",
                "username": "BenjaminFair"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 5:\n\n(1 comment)\n\nWould it be possible to do this using Intel's x86-power-control service? It looks like that should \"just work\" since your GPIOs are configured as PGOOD and POWER_UP_PIN already.\n\nhttps://github.com/openbmc/x86-power-control"
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1560305776,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Lisa Liu",
                "email": "liuyj19@lenovo.com",
                "username": "liuyj19"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 5:\n\n> Patch Set 5:\n> \n> (1 comment)\n> \n> Would it be possible to do this using Intel's x86-power-control service? It looks like that should \"just work\" since your GPIOs are configured as PGOOD and POWER_UP_PIN already.\n> \n> https://github.com/openbmc/x86-power-control\n\nI know this Intel's service,I once used it, but build this service with \"obmc-op-control-power\" together, there are errors: \"the file /lib/systemd/system/op-reset-chassis-on@.service and file /lib/systemd/system/op-reset-chassis-running@.service conflict between attempted installs of obmc-op-control-power-1.0+git0+c8334f3258-r1.armv6 and x86-power-control-1.0+git0+2c714052d6-r1.armv6\". If remove \"obmc-op-control-power\", it can't use ipmitool chassis command."
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1560307748,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Benjamin Fair",
                "email": "benjaminfair@google.com",
                "username": "BenjaminFair"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 5:\n\n> Patch Set 5:\n> \n> > Patch Set 5:\n> > \n> > (1 comment)\n> > \n> > Would it be possible to do this using Intel's x86-power-control service? It looks like that should \"just work\" since your GPIOs are configured as PGOOD and POWER_UP_PIN already.\n> > \n> > https://github.com/openbmc/x86-power-control\n> \n> I know this Intel's service,I once used it, but build this service with \"obmc-op-control-power\" together, there are errors: \"the file /lib/systemd/system/op-reset-chassis-on@.service and file /lib/systemd/system/op-reset-chassis-running@.service conflict between attempted installs of obmc-op-control-power-1.0+git0+c8334f3258-r1.armv6 and x86-power-control-1.0+git0+2c714052d6-r1.armv6\". If remove \"obmc-op-control-power\", it can't use ipmitool chassis command.\n\nThat's surprising. Both obmc-op-control-power and x86-power-control implement the same interface so you should be able to switch out one for the other.\n\nCan you push your code to use x86-power-control to a branch on GitHub so I can try to figure out what the problem is?"
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1560311191,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Patrick Venture",
                "email": "venture@google.com",
                "username": "pstrinkle"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 5:\n\nNote: We have the same services, scripts, etc.  I think the gpioutil can be used for some of it, we have an open action item from the last OCP summit to look into converting from shell scripts to gpioutil calls from the services."
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1561386183,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Lisa Liu",
                "email": "liuyj19@lenovo.com",
                "username": "liuyj19"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 5:\n\n> Patch Set 5:\n> \n> > Patch Set 5:\n> > \n> > > Patch Set 5:\n> > > \n> > > (1 comment)\n> > > \n> > > Would it be possible to do this using Intel's x86-power-control service? It looks like that should \"just work\" since your GPIOs are configured as PGOOD and POWER_UP_PIN already.\n> > > \n> > > https://github.com/openbmc/x86-power-control\n> > \n> > I know this Intel's service,I once used it, but build this service with \"obmc-op-control-power\" together, there are errors: \"the file /lib/systemd/system/op-reset-chassis-on@.service and file /lib/systemd/system/op-reset-chassis-running@.service conflict between attempted installs of obmc-op-control-power-1.0+git0+c8334f3258-r1.armv6 and x86-power-control-1.0+git0+2c714052d6-r1.armv6\". If remove \"obmc-op-control-power\", it can't use ipmitool chassis command.\n> \n> That's surprising. Both obmc-op-control-power and x86-power-control implement the same interface so you should be able to switch out one for the other.\n> \n> Can you push your code to use x86-power-control to a branch on GitHub so I can try to figure out what the problem is?\n\nBenjamin, I have push our code which use x86-power-control to a branch on our GitHub and send the website of GitHub to you via email on June 12, Could you help to check it? Thanks!"
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1562715372,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Benjamin Fair",
                "email": "benjaminfair@google.com",
                "username": "BenjaminFair"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 5:\n\n> Patch Set 5:\n> \n> > Patch Set 5:\n> > \n> > > Patch Set 5:\n> > > \n> > > > Patch Set 5:\n> > > > \n> > > > (1 comment)\n> > > > \n> > > > Would it be possible to do this using Intel's x86-power-control service? It looks like that should \"just work\" since your GPIOs are configured as PGOOD and POWER_UP_PIN already.\n> > > > \n> > > > https://github.com/openbmc/x86-power-control\n> > > \n> > > I know this Intel's service,I once used it, but build this service with \"obmc-op-control-power\" together, there are errors: \"the file /lib/systemd/system/op-reset-chassis-on@.service and file /lib/systemd/system/op-reset-chassis-running@.service conflict between attempted installs of obmc-op-control-power-1.0+git0+c8334f3258-r1.armv6 and x86-power-control-1.0+git0+2c714052d6-r1.armv6\". If remove \"obmc-op-control-power\", it can't use ipmitool chassis command.\n> > \n> > That's surprising. Both obmc-op-control-power and x86-power-control implement the same interface so you should be able to switch out one for the other.\n> > \n> > Can you push your code to use x86-power-control to a branch on GitHub so I can try to figure out what the problem is?\n> \n> Benjamin, I have push our code which use x86-power-control to a branch on our GitHub and send the website of GitHub to you via email on June 12, Could you help to check it? Thanks!\n\nWhile trying to debug this, I came across this issue comment:\nhttps://github.com/openbmc/openbmc/issues/3561#issuecomment-502159687\n\nIt sounds like x86-power-control isn't being kept up to date currently, so just using the scripts is fine.\n\nPlease still make sure that the different powerctrl recipes for each platform have unique names and act as virtual providers. Let me know if you need help with how to set this up."
        },
